{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794759528,"nonce":"eELActHZoEU/ze1tEHZnEVIMlVU=","title":"cn demo visual check, 2026-11-15: CPU use by widget and a scroll jump, as reported","message":"Reported by the owner from a window of the cn demo (release build of HEAD plus the day's commits). CPU is the share of one core, as read by the owner.\n\n- indeterminate progress animation: 35%\n- active scroll: up to 20%\n- switch toggle during its transition: 20%\n- slider: 65%\n- popover: 23%\n- number gauges: 40%\n- radio selection: 14%\n- dropdown select: 5 to 8%\n- calendar: 25% while browsing, 55% on a continuous swipe, and the day numbers vibrate or flicker\n- a dropdown left open, window idle: 27%\n- animations in overlays run entirely on the CPU (judged from popovers)\n- hovering between menubar items jumps the page's scroll back to the top\n\nKnown so far. Two causes found and fixed, see the two issues filed with this one (a never-painted scroll container making every frame a full paint; an open overlay kept drawing by an infinite animation elsewhere). A headless reproduction of the menubar jump (menubar_hover_scroll, a hover menubar in a scrolled page, all three paint modes) keeps the scroll at its offset and does not reproduce it; the window has something the session lacks. The indeterminate bar in a window of its own over 300 cn rows measured 0.07 to 0.16 cores, not 35%. Nothing here is measured against the fixes in a window of the real demo.\n\nWhat a time-only or hover animation on this page costs is dominated by what a full paint costs (about 8 ms of render in the headless session) when the frame is not served from the cache, which is the reason to find every gate that forces one.\n\nLabels: bug, perf, area:app.","files":null}]}